{Ruy Lopez: Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Defense. Theory ends about here. From this point the game is decided by understanding rather than memory.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. O-O Bd7 {Both sides have what they wanted from the opening. The middlegame will be about the centre.} 6. c4 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 ! Nf6 {Here the exchange is tempting and wrong: trading the good bishop leaves the dark squares bare.} (8... a5 Na3 d5 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Qb3 Rb8 10. d3 Nd7 11. Bxc6 bxc6 ?! 12. Qa4 Rb6 13. b4 c5 14. a3 cxb4 15. axb4 Qb8 16. Be3 Rxb4 17. Qxa6 Qb7 18. Nc3 c6 19. Qxb7 Rxb7 20. Ra8+ Rb8 21. Rfa1 Kd8 22. Rxb8+ Nxb8 23. Ra8 Kc8 24. Na4 c5 25. Ra7 Nd7 26. Bd2 Be7 27. Nc3 Bd8 28. Nd5 Re8 29. f4 f6 30. f5 Rf8 31. Ra6 Bc7 {The endgame is winning but not simple — the passed pawn needs its king, not just its rook.} 32. Rc6 1-0
